    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>This <strong>DeskCycle Ellipse review</strong> looks at a seated under desk elliptical built by the makers of the popular DeskCycle bike. Its selling points are a low pedal height that fits under more desks, and a wider magnetic resistance range than most seated ellipticals. You use it in your chair, pedal in an oval motion, and adjust the effort from easy to properly tiring.</p>
<p>Based on specs and owner reviews, the DeskCycle Ellipse is one of the best choices if you want real resistance, have a lower desk, or found smaller machines too easy. It is larger and costs more than budget models, and like every seated elliptical it needs a little setup to stop it sliding.</p>

<h2>What Is the DeskCycle Ellipse?</h2>
<p>The DeskCycle Ellipse is a manual seated elliptical. Two pedals travel in an oval path driven by a magnetic resistance system, and a display tracks your activity, including calories and distance. There is no motor: your legs do the work, and there is nothing to plug in.</p>
<p>People sometimes search for it as the "desk cycle ellipse" or "ellipse desk cycle," which makes sense, because it comes from the same company as the DeskCycle 2 under desk bike. The Ellipse is the elliptical version: same low-profile idea, different foot path.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Quick verdict:</strong> the DeskCycle Ellipse is for people who want more than gentle fidgeting. If you plan to push the resistance or your desk is low, it is one of the strongest seated options available. If you only want gentle movement, a smaller machine will do.</p></div>

<h2>DeskCycle Ellipse Review: What Stands Out</h2>
<h3>Low pedal height</h3>
<p>This is the feature that matters most. On any seated elliptical, your knees rise at the top of the stride. A lower pedal path means your knees rise less, so the Ellipse clears desks that taller machines hit. If you have had a Cubii or budget model bump the underside of your desk, this is the fix you are looking for.</p>
<h3>Wide resistance range</h3>
<p>Most seated ellipticals top out at a level fit users find easy. The DeskCycle Ellipse's magnetic system scales from very light, for pedaling while typing, to hard enough that you will notice it in your quads and glutes. That makes it useful for more of your day, and it gives you room to progress. Our list of the best <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk ellipticals with resistance</a> compares it with other high-range models.</p>
<h3>Quiet magnetic drive</h3>
<p>Magnetic resistance means no friction pads and no rubbing. Owners regularly report using it through calls and video meetings. It is not totally silent, but at normal speeds it is quiet enough for an open office or a shared home.</p>
<h3>Smooth elliptical motion</h3>
<p>The oval foot path keeps your feet supported throughout the stride. Compared with a pedal bike, there is less of a sharp knee bend at the top, which many people with sensitive knees prefer.</p>

<h2>What Using It Every Day Is Like</h2>
<p>A DeskCycle Ellipse review is only useful if it tells you how the machine fits into a normal workday, so here is the typical pattern owners describe. In the morning, while you are clearing email, the resistance stays low and the pedaling fades into the background. You notice it about as much as tapping your foot.</p>
<p>During calls or when you are reading a long document, many owners turn the dial up for ten or fifteen minutes. This is where the Ellipse separates itself from smaller machines: there is enough resistance left to make those blocks feel like exercise rather than fidgeting. Later in the afternoon, when energy dips, a short harder block can help you feel more alert.</p>
<p>Over weeks, the main change owners mention is moving up the resistance scale as their legs adapt. That progression is the real reason to pay more for the Ellipse. A machine you outgrow in a month tends to end up in a closet.</p>
<h3>Is it good for seniors and rehab?</h3>
<p>The smooth motion and very light low settings make it suitable for many older users and people easing back into activity. The wide range simply means there is room to progress. If you are recovering from surgery or managing a joint condition, check with your doctor or physical therapist about settings and session length first. For motorized options, see our <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> guide.</p>

<h2>The Downsides</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Larger footprint.</strong> It takes more floor space than mini ellipticals. Measure the area under your desk, including room for your chair.</li>
<li><strong>Higher price.</strong> It costs more than budget seated ellipticals. Check the current price, because it can vary noticeably.</li>
<li><strong>It still slides.</strong> At higher resistance you push harder, so a hard floor needs a mat or something to brace it against.</li>
<li><strong>Seated only.</strong> It is not designed for standing use.</li>
<li><strong>Overkill for light use.</strong> If you only ever want easy pedaling, you are paying for resistance you will not use.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Will the DeskCycle Ellipse Fit Under Your Desk?</h2>
<p>Because the low profile is the Ellipse's big advantage, it is worth confirming the fit for your own setup. Your desk height, chair height and leg length all matter. Use the checker below with two quick measurements.</p>
[[tool:clearance]]
<p>If the result is close, lower your chair slightly and sit a little further back. Both give your knees more room at the top of the stride. For more on seating, our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-under-desk-elliptical/">how to use an under desk elliptical</a> covers chair choice and posture.</p>

