<h2>How Hard Can You Work on It?</h2>
<p>At light resistance, the Ellipse feels like the easy background pedaling you get from any seated elliptical, roughly 2.0 METs in the Compendium of Physical Activities. Push the resistance up and you move toward moderate effort, around 2.8 METs, where you will feel warm and your legs will know about it.</p>
<p>In practical terms, most people burn roughly 100 to 200 calories an hour depending on effort and body weight. Try your own numbers in the <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a>. A good pattern is light resistance while you type and short higher-resistance blocks while you read or listen.</p>

<h2>Setup Tips for the DeskCycle Ellipse</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Brace or mat it.</strong> Higher resistance means harder pushes. A [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] and a desk leg or wall behind it keep it from creeping.</li>
<li><strong>Lock the chair.</strong> A chair without casters is ideal. Otherwise, add [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]].</li>
<li><strong>Place the display where you can see it.</strong> Glancing at your stats is part of what keeps you going.</li>
<li><strong>Increase resistance gradually.</strong> Your quads will notice the higher settings. Build up over a couple of weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Use intervals.</strong> Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> makes it easy to alternate easy and hard blocks.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Who Should Buy the DeskCycle Ellipse</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buy it if</strong> your knees hit the desk on other seated ellipticals.</li>
<li><strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a seated machine that can actually tire you out.</li>
<li><strong>Buy it if</strong> you want quiet magnetic resistance without a motor or a power cord.</li>
<li><strong>Skip it if</strong> you need the smallest possible machine, or you only want light fidgeting.</li>
<li><strong>Skip it if</strong> you want to stand; look at a compact strider instead.</li>
</ul>
<p>Want to know what owners say after months of use? Our companion page on <a href="/deskcycle-ellipse-under-desk-elliptical-reviews/">DeskCycle Ellipse under desk elliptical reviews</a> collects recurring praise, complaints and troubleshooting tips.</p>

<h2>Verdict: DeskCycle Ellipse Review Summary</h2>
<p>The DeskCycle Ellipse earns its place by solving two problems that plague seated ellipticals: knees hitting the desk, and resistance that runs out too soon. It is quiet, smooth and adjustable enough to grow with you. You pay for that with a larger footprint and a higher price than budget models.</p>
<p>If those trade-offs work for you, it is one of the best seated ellipticals you can buy. If you want something smaller and simpler, the Cubii JR2 remains our best all-round pick. Still deciding? Take the <a href="/tools/which-under-desk-elliptical-quiz/">60-second quiz</a> or see our ranked list of the <a href="/what-is-the-best-under-desk-elliptical/">best under desk ellipticals</a>.</p>
